The SystemOS union on "/" is one volume to macOS. The owner chain union-mounts the SystemOS volume over the root
volume, so files under "/" come from two APFS volumes and getfsstat lists two mounts on "/"; macOS CoreServices
(FileIDTree) aborts on two volumes with one device id or on a device id no mount lists (macOS-DroidVM's
virt-union-single-dev and virt-union-hide-lower meet the same). On XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX the root volume under the union is
reported as the union, as DroidVM's virt_union_top does: getfsstat leaves the root volume out, and stat, statfs and
getattrlist give the SystemOS volume's device id and statfs (with MNT_UNION, which libc's opendir needs to merge and
deduplicate the two layers; reported the other way round, readdir listed the upper layer only, macOS run33).
--- a/bsd/vfs/vfs_subr.c
+++ b/bsd/vfs/vfs_subr.c
@@ -13504,3 +13504,24 @@
 {
 	return os_atomic_load(&vp->v_ext_flag, relaxed) & VE_APPENDONLY;
 }
+
+#if XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX
+/*
+ * The volume macOS sees at "/". The owner chain union-mounts the SystemOS volume over the root volume's "/", so files
+ * there come from two APFS volumes with two device ids, and getfsstat lists two mounts on "/". macOS CoreServices
+ * (FileIDTree) identifies a volume by its device id and aborts on a second volume with the same one or on a device id
+ * no mount lists. As macOS-DroidVM's virt_union_top does, the root volume under the union is reported as the union:
+ * getfsstat leaves the root volume out and stat, statfs and getattrlist give the SystemOS volume's device id and statfs,
+ * which keeps MNT_UNION, so libc's opendir reads a directory of both layers whole and drops the duplicate names.
+ */
+mount_t
+native_union_identity(mount_t mp)
+{
+	mount_t top = rootvnode != NULLVP ? rootvnode->v_mount : NULL;
+	if (mp != NULL && top != NULL && top != mp && (top->mnt_flag & MNT_UNION) && top->mnt_vnodecovered != NULLVP &&
+	    top->mnt_vnodecovered->v_mount == mp && (mp->mnt_flag & MNT_ROOTFS)) {
+		return top;
+	}
+	return mp;
+}
+#endif /* XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX */
